WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.807
Many datasets arrive in a wide format because that layout is easy for people to read in a spreadsheet.

00:00:07.807 --> 00:00:12.782
Here, the months January, February, and March are separate columns.

00:00:12.782 --> 00:00:17.997
That may look convenient, but it can make grouping and plotting more difficult.

00:00:17.997 --> 00:00:22.042
Melt converts those month columns into rows.

00:00:22.042 --> 00:00:26.953
After melting, we have one month column and one sales column.

00:00:27.524 --> 00:00:33.066
This is called long format, and long format is often better for analysis.

00:00:33.066 --> 00:00:39.281
It works naturally with grouping, plotting libraries, and many statistical tools.

00:00:39.281 --> 00:00:48.101
A useful way to think about melt is this: the values that are currently stored in column names become values inside a

00:00:48.101 --> 00:00:53.521
new column. Run the example and compare the number of rows before and after melting.

00:00:53.521 --> 00:01:00.196
Then imagine a spreadsheet with twelve month columns or fifty survey question columns.

00:01:00.196 --> 00:01:05.503
Melt can turn that wide structure into a much more analysis-friendly form.
